# OpenCode PR loop before merge

Before an agent merges a pull request in this repository, run the `opencode-pr-loop` skill to completion on that PR. Read the skill and follow it in full, including thread replies, suppressed findings, and the clean-review stop.

## Review model

Use OpenCode's default model for every `/codereview` in the loop: `opencode run "/codereview PR_URL auto-post"` with no `-m`. Record that as `REVIEW_MODEL` and reuse it for every iteration. Do not ask which model to use. Model choice does not need HITL approval.

If the user already named a model in the request, use that model instead. If the selected model fails, stop and ask which model to switch to. Do not substitute another model silently.

This rule is the standing merge grant for this repository. It narrows the global HITL merge rule only in the case below.

## When the agent may merge

Merge without a separate in-chat "merge it" when all of these hold:

- This PR's `opencode-pr-loop` run posted `OpenCode-PR-Loop — DONE`.
- That DONE cites a clean OpenCode review of the current head SHA: an explicit all-clear, or no new comments with zero unresolved OpenCode threads and zero untriaged suppressed findings.
- PR checks pass, no reviewer has requested changes, and the user has not asked to hold the PR.
- The base branch is not a production or release branch. Those merges still need an explicit in-chat approval.

Merge with the strategy the repository expects. Leave auto-merge off.

## When HITL is still required

Wait for an explicit in-chat merge approval for this PR when any of these are true:

- The loop has not been run on this PR.
- The loop stopped before `DONE` (iteration cap, auth failure, unclear review, or another blocker).
- The latest OpenCode review is not clean.
- Commits landed after the SHA covered by the DONE review.

Opening a PR, pushing, and waiting on CI stay allowed. Those steps do not authorize a merge. A DONE on a different PR does not carry over.

## Examples

```text
# BAD — green CI, then gh pr merge, with the loop never run
# BAD — loop hits the iteration cap, then gh pr merge
# BAD — DONE on abc123, new commits land, then gh pr merge
# BAD — pause the loop to ask which review model to use
# GOOD — opencode run "/codereview PR_URL auto-post" with no -m, every iteration
# GOOD — loop posts DONE on the current head, checks pass, then gh pr merge
# GOOD — loop stops short of DONE; report that and wait for an explicit "merge it"
```
